We evaluate on a set of 8 occupations: 4 male-skewed ( lawyer, physician, developer, politician ) and 4 female-skewed ( social worker, hairdresser, cashier, receptionist ), generating 10 images per occupation for k ∈ { 2 , 3 } k{\in}\{2,3\} persons with randomly assigned target demographics per detected instance.
We report five metrics: race and gender steering accuracy ( Acc race \mathrm{Acc}_{\mathrm{race}} , Acc gender \mathrm{Acc}_{\mathrm{gender}} ) against the assigned target; aesthetic score delta ( A ​ e ​ s ​ Δ Aes\Delta ) [ 33 ] relative to the unsteered baseline;
background preservation ( LPIPS bg \mathrm{LPIPS}_{\mathrm{bg}} [ 45 ] ) outside the instance masks; and prompt–image CLIP similarity delta ( CLIP ​ Δ \mathrm{CLIP}\Delta ), where values closer to zero indicate less semantic drift.

